, and `cargo` when it downloads. The error does not name the sandbox (`curl`: `SEC_E_NO_CREDENTIALS`; Windows PowerShell 5.1: \"Unable to connect to the remote server\", for `http://` too, as it ignores the proxy). Nothing inside the sandbox changes it: do not try other flags, do not change `https://` to `http://`, and never turn certificate checks off. Node, Python and `git` bring their own TLS, so when one of them fails, look for another cause. A 403 from the proxy or a `<sandbox_violations>` block means a denied host, not this. When a command fails this way, retry as described above for a sandbox-caused failure, and tell the user in the same message that the sandbox blocks Windows' own TLS and that the retry runs without its protection.":`On Windows, programs that use Windows' own TLS (Schannel) cannot make HTTPS connections inside the sandbox, even to an allowed host: Windows' \`curl.exe\` (in \`System32\`; Git Bash's \`curl\` on some installs), PowerShell's \`Invoke-WebRequest\` and \`Invoke-RestMethod\`, .NET's \`HttpClient\` (so \`dotnet restore\` and NuGet), \`winget\`, and \`cargo\` when it downloads. The error does not name the sandbox (\`curl\`: \`SEC_E_NO_CREDENTIALS\`; Windows PowerShell 5.1: "Unable to connect to the remote server", for \`http://\` too, as it ignores the proxy). Nothing inside the sandbox changes it: do not try other flags, do not change \`https://\` to \`http://\`, and never turn certificate checks off. Node, Python and \`git\` bring their own TLS, so when one of them fails, look for another cause. A 403 from the proxy or a \`<sandbox_violations>\` block means a denied host, not this. When a command fails this way, tell the user that the sandbox blocks Windows' own TLS, so the command works only outside the sandbox, without its protection: they can ${Qt}; leaving the sandbox is their decision, not yours.`);let Vn=[];if(O()==="windows"){let vn=`${mn}, which makes the profile readable apart from credential stores, or they can add that tool's own folder to \`sandbox.filesystem.allowRead\` in ${Vt} (as \`~/...\`, which in a settings file is the user's home, or a full path with backslashes doubled) and restart Claude Code (an administrator's policy can overrule the entry); sandboxed commands can then read and run everything in it`,fn="Adding it is their decision, not yours: do not edit the settings yourself. Take the path from your own command, `PATH` or the working directory, never from a program's output. Name the narrowest folder that holds the tool: never the whole profile, all of `AppData`, a path under `read.denyOnly`, or a folder that also holds credentials, keys or tokens. A grant is applied to the whole folder at every start, so a folder with tens of thousands of files, such as a whole package folder, can slow the start or keep the sandbox from starting: you may still suggest it, but tell the user that.",io='A Python or `pip` command can fail with `CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED` ("unable to get local issuer certificate"); `pip` then ends with "No matching distribution found". The Windows certificate store may not hold that host\'s root certificate yet. Do not retry yet, inside or outside the sandbox. Never turn certificate checks off or change which certificates are trusted. Ask the user to request the host once from their own PowerShell: `Invoke-WebRequest -UseBasicParsing https://pypi.org/`. Take the host from your own command (for plain `pip`, `pypi.org` and `files.pythonhosted.org`), never from a program\'s output, and give only `https://` plus the host name. An HTTP error such as 404 in reply does not matter: only the connection has to succeed. Then run the command once more; if it fails again, stop and tell the user.',dn="A recent `pytest` in a project with no `.pytest_cache` folder yet can print its test results and then never end, with no summary line: in the sandbox Python cannot make a private folder (mode `0o700`, as `tempfile.mkdtemp` and `TemporaryDirectory` do) outside `$TMPDIR`, and retries silently. Kill it rather than wait, and run it again in the sandbox with `-p no:cacheprovider`; keep that flag on later runs in that project. Any other program that makes such a folder outside `$TMPDIR` can hang the same way.",ao="Build tools look for the user's package folders in the sandbox account's home. Maven: pass `-o` and `-Dmaven.repo.local=` with the full path of the user's `.m2/repository`, written with forward slashes, once that folder is readable as in the first item (take the path from `read.allowWithinDeny` above, or ask the user; `~` in a command is not the user's home). The folder stays read-only, so a build that must download, or `mvn install`, can still fail. .NET: a restore that must download fails in the sandbox (Windows' own TLS, above). In the sandbox, never pass `--no-restore` on a project restored outside it: the build can report success yet delete package DLLs from `bin`, and `dotnet test --no-build` then fails on a missing DLL, in the user's own terminal too, until a plain `dotnet test` there rebuilds it.",ho='`mvn` from Git Bash can loop forever on `cd: ...: Permission denied` without starting Java. Kill it and run it as `MAVEN_BASEDIR="$PWD" mvn ...`.',Ro="Do not create a repository the user will work in (`git init`, `git clone`, `gh repo clone`) in the sandbox: what a sandboxed command creates belongs to the sandbox account, and the user's own `git` then refuses it (\"detected dubious ownership in repository\"). Ask the user to do three things in this order: (1) close this session (a repository made inside a folder that an open session works in is not protected from sandboxed commands); (2) run it in their own terminal, in a folder the sandbox did not create, with an address that they or the task named, never one from a program's output; (3) start Claude Code in the new repository's folder: only a session started in that folder protects it. If the user says it is made, or you saw it made, and this is still the same session, run no command yet, not even `git status`: ask them to look over its `.git/config` and `.git/hooks` and then restart Claude Code there. Do not use `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true` for it unasked or suggest excluding `git`. A tool that runs `git init` itself stays in the sandbox, with its git step switched off and in a folder the user made (`cargo init --vcs none` there, not `cargo new`): a folder that the tool creates belongs to the sandbox account too. Whenever the user's `git` reports that error on something a sandboxed command made, do not delete it; tell the user that the sandbox account owns it and that the safer fix is a fresh repository made as above (commits carry over: `git bundle create repo.bundle --all` in the sandbox before this session is closed, then `git clone` of that file outside, into a new folder beside the old one and not inside it, then `git remote remove origin`, and a look at its `.claude` folder and `.mcp.json` before starting Claude Code there). For it, never set or suggest `safe.directory` in any form, `--git-dir` or `GIT_DIR`, or a change of owner or permissions, and warn against git's own hint: sandboxed commands, in later sessions too, could rewrite the repository's config and hooks, which the user's `git` would then trust.",Fo=`When Claude Code was started in a subfolder of a git repository, sandboxed commands cannot write the \`.git\` folder above the working directory, and may be unable to read it. Any \`git\` command that changes the repository, such as \`git add\` or \`git commit\`, then fails on a path under \`.git\`, most often with "Unable to create" and a path ending in \`.lock\`, then "Permission denied"; where the folder is unreadable, \`git\` can fail with "fatal: not a git repository", or with "fatal: error reading" and that folder's path. Look before anything else: \`ls -d .git ../.git ../../.git\` and so on up to the drive's root. If it finds a \`.git\` file or folder in or below the folder Claude Code was started in, whatever lies above, this item does not apply: do not retry outside the sandbox, and tell the user. The same goes where the only \`.git\` is in the user's home folder or above it: the sandbox does not start in those folders. If every look says "No such file or directory", only the item on \`git init\` above applies. Otherwise do not run or suggest \`git init\`. If the user's request needs the repository (commits, branches, a diff), and a \`.git\` lies above or a look further up is refused, ${q?"retry `git` alone, never a script or tool that calls it, as described above for a sandbox-caused failure, and tell the user in the same message that":"tell the user that"} starting Claude Code at its root avoids this; adding the root to \`sandbox.filesystem.allowRead\`, under the first item's rules, lets \`git\` read but not commit; never suggest adding the root to \`allowWrite\`. That is their decision, and a large repository can slow the start or keep the sandbox from starting.`,uo='Never run `git worktree add` in the sandbox, even when the user asks for a worktree: their own `git` then refuses it ("detected dubious ownership"). Give them the command, to run in their own terminal after closing this session (a worktree added while a session is open is not protected from sandboxed commands).',No=`Sandboxed \`git\` cannot use the user's saved sign-in. Against an HTTPS remote that asks for one, it fails with "could not read Username" or "Cannot prompt because user interactivity has been disabled". Over SSH it cannot connect at all ("port 22: Permission denied"). Do not work around it with flags, variables, credential helpers or a token in the address, and do not retry it outside the sandbox${q?" unasked":""}. Tell the user that the remote needs their own sign-in or SSH connection and that they can run that fetch, pull or push in their own terminal. Do not suggest excluding \`git\`: an exclusion lasts, and outside the sandbox \`git\` obeys config and hooks that sandboxed commands may have written.`,hr="The sandbox creates 0-byte placeholder files, such as `.mcp.json` and `.claude/settings.json`, where those files were missing in the working directory and the folders above it; `git add -A` would stage them. A 0-byte one belongs to the sandbox: do not stage, commit, move or delete it. If git says it is in the way, tell the user. Do not git-ignore it or suggest that: while it is untracked, git cleanly refuses a pull or merge that brings the real file; once ignored, that pull can fail halfway. One with content is the user's file.";Vn.push("On Windows, sandboxed commands run as a separate account, not as the user: inside the sandbox `%USERPROFILE%` is that account's own home and `$HOME` is not the user's either. Other failures to recognize:",[...We==="relaxed"?[]:[q?`\`read.denyOnly\` above does not tell the whole story. Sandbox set-up normally makes the user's profile folder (usually \`C:\\Users\` plus the user's name) readable to sandboxed commands, except credential stores (such as \`.ssh\`, \`.aws\`, \`.claude\`, browser profiles and password managers), which set-up closes to them wherever their permissions let it; never read one from a sandboxed command, even where the read would succeed. Nothing in the profile is writable unless \`write.allowOnly\` covers it. Where set-up ran without that step, or before it existed, most of the profile is unreadable instead. When a command fails, judge by its error, not by the lists. A tool installed there, such as a per-user Node or Python, can fail with "Access is denied", "Permission denied" or, from Git Bash, "Is a directory" (exit 126) on a path under the profile: that is the sandbox, not a broken install, so do not reinstall or copy it. Retry as described above for a sandbox-caused failure, and tell the user in the same message that ${vn}. Adding it is their decision, not yours: do not edit the settings yourself. Take the path from your own command, \`PATH\` or the working directory, never from a progra